ML_PAGE("l4-prep", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l4\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">CHAPTER 4 · MCQ PREPARATION</span><h2>Chapter 4 MCQ Prep</h2><p>The complete MCQ preparation notes in their original order: 40 multiple-choice questions sit inside the sections they belong to, with the answer, the logic and the exam trap hidden until you reveal them.</p></header><div class=\"study-grid three-col\"><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>40</strong><span>MCQs in the notes</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>40</strong><span>Exam traps explained</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>Difficult-mode questions</span></div></div><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l4\" data-quiz=\"4\">Start Chapter 4 simulator</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l4\" id=\"l4p-toc\"><summary><strong>MCQ notes</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">4 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li class=\"toc-group\">PART A - APPENDIX 4A QUESTIONS</li><li data-full=\"Section A - Appendix 4A: Time Value of Money (Q1-Q20)\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s2\" title=\"Section A - Appendix 4A: Time Value of Money (Q1-Q20)\">Section A: TVM (Q1-Q20)</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART B - CHAPTER 4 QUESTIONS</li><li data-full=\"Section B - Chapter 4: Return and Risk (Q21-Q40)\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s4\" title=\"Section B - Chapter 4: Return and Risk (Q21-Q40)\">Section B: return and risk (Q21-Q40)</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART C - FORMULAS AND REVIEW</li><li data-full=\"Essential formula sheet\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s6\" title=\"Essential formula sheet\">Essential formula sheet</button></li><li data-full=\"How to interpret your exam mistakes\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s7\" title=\"How to interpret your exam mistakes\">How to interpret your exam mistakes</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"prep-notes theme-l4\" open><summary><div><strong>Chapter 4 MCQ preparation</strong><small>Exam strategy, practice questions, formula sheets and review - section by section, answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body compact\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>4 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><h2>Chapter 4 + Appendix 4A - Combined MCQ Exam</h2>\n<p>Fundamentals of Investing · Return, Risk &amp; Time Value of Money</p>\n<p>This combined exam tests two closely connected questions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Appendix 4A: How do we compare cash flows received at different times?</li>\n<li>Chapter 4: How do we determine whether an investment's return adequately compensates for its risk?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>I've based the exam on your Chapter 4 and Appendix 4A slides, including their worked examples and risk classifications.</p>\n<p><strong>Questions:</strong> 40 · <strong>Suggested time:</strong> 60 min · <strong>Target score:</strong> 80%</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Section A - Appendix 4A</strong> (20 questions): Simple and compound interest, PV/FV, annuities, mixed streams, compounding frequency and investment comparisons.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Section B - Chapter 4</strong> (20 questions): HPR, required return, IRR, growth rates, risk classification, standard deviation, coefficient of variation and investment decisions.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The questions progress from applying formulas to analysing competing alternatives and evaluating financial claims. Some wrong answers are deliberately plausible because they reflect common misunderstandings.</p>\n<p>Use a calculator if needed. Round numerical results to two decimal places unless the question states otherwise.</p><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l4p-d1\">PART A - APPENDIX 4A QUESTIONS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Section A - Appendix 4A: Time Value of Money (Q1-Q20)</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><div><p>An investor can receive RM1,000 today or RM1,080 one year from now. Assuming an available risk-free annual return of 8% and no other differences, which statement is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM1,000 today is worth more.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM1,080 next year is worth more.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The two alternatives are financially equivalent.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The alternatives cannot be compared using interest rates.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>RM1,000 today can grow to RM1,000 x 1.08 = RM1,080 after one year. They are equivalent at an 8% discount rate under the question's assumptions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Compare cash flows at the same point in time rather than their nominal amounts.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><div><p>RM1,000 is deposited at 10% simple interest per year for three years. What is its future value?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM1,300</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM1,331</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM1,210</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM1,100</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Simple interest is earned only on the original principal. Annual interest = RM1,000 x 10% = RM100. After three years, the balance is RM1,000 + 3(RM100) = RM1,300.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not compound interest when the question explicitly specifies simple interest.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><div><p>The same RM1,000 is invested at 10% compounded annually for three years. What is the future value?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM1,300</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM1,331</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM1,330</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM1,210</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>FV = RM1,000(1.10)^3 = RM1,331. Unlike simple interest, previously accumulated interest earns additional interest.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> RM1,300 is the simple-interest answer, not the compound-interest answer.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><div><p>An investor requires 10% annually. How much should they deposit today to receive RM1,210 exactly two years from now?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM1,100</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM1,210</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM1,000</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM1,331</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>PV = RM1,210/(1.10)^2 = RM1,000. Present value is obtained by reversing the compounding process.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Future money must be discounted, not multiplied by the compound growth factor.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><div><p>RM3,000 is invested for four years at 7% compounded annually. What will it be worth at the end?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM3,840.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM3,210.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM3,932.39</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM4,207.00</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>FV = RM3,000(1.07)^4 = RM3,932.39. The exponent represents four compounding periods.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> RM3,840 is the simple-interest result; the question specifies compounding.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><div><p>Two analysts value the same fixed future cash flow, but Analyst A uses a 5% discount rate and Analyst B uses 12%. Which conclusion is correct, all else equal?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Analyst B obtains a higher present value.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Both must obtain identical present values.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The discount rate cannot affect present value.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Analyst A obtains a higher present value.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>PV = FV/(1+r)^n. A higher discount rate increases the denominator and therefore reduces the present value of a positive future cash flow.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Compounding and discounting respond in opposite directions to increasing rates.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q7</span><div><p>Which cash-flow pattern qualifies as an ordinary annuity?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM1,000 at the end of every year for five years</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM1,000 immediately followed by RM1,000 annually starting next year</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Unequal payments at the end of every year</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM1,000 at irregular dates</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>An ordinary annuity consists of equal cash flows occurring at equal intervals, with each payment at the end of the period. A payment immediately at time zero does not follow the ordinary-annuity timing convention.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Check both equality of cash flows and when each payment occurs.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q8</span><div><p>An investor deposits RM1,000 at the end of each year for three years into an account earning 10% annually. What is the future value at the end of Year 3?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM3,000</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM3,310</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM3,641</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM3,300</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>FV = RM1,000[(1.10^3 - 1)/0.10] = RM3,310. Alternatively, RM1,000(1.10)^2 + RM1,000(1.10) + RM1,000 = RM3,310.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The deposit at the end of Year 3 has zero additional years to compound.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q9</span><div><p>An investment pays RM110 at the end of Year 1 and RM110 at the end of Year 2. What is the present value at a 10% annual discount rate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM200.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM181.82</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM190.91</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM220.00</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>PV = RM110/1.10 + RM110/(1.10)^2 = RM100 + RM90.91 = RM190.91. The two payments have different discounting horizons.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Equal cash flows do not have equal present values when received at different dates.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q10</span><div><p>An investment pays RM100 in Year 1, RM200 in Year 2 and RM300 in Year 3. Using a 10% annual discount rate, what is its present value?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM600.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM545.45</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM500.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM481.59</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>PV = 100/1.10 + 200/(1.10)^2 + 300/(1.10)^3 = RM481.59. Unequal payments are discounted individually before summing.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not use the ordinary-annuity formula because these cash flows are unequal.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q11</span><div><p>You must choose between RM1,000 immediately and RM1,200 in three years. Assuming an 8% annual required return and otherwise identical risk, which is financially preferable?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM1,200 in three years, because its nominal amount is higher.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM1,000 immediately, because RM1,200 in three years has a present value of about RM952.60.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Both are equivalent at an 8% rate.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The answer depends only on the investor's age.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>PV of RM1,200 = RM1,200/(1.08)^3 ≈ RM952.60, which is less than RM1,000 now. Therefore the immediate payment is more valuable at the stated required return.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A larger future payment can have a smaller present value.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q12</span><div><p>Investor A invests RM8,000 immediately. Investor B invests RM2,000 at each year-end for four years. Both earn the same positive compound return. At the end of Year 4, who has more wealth?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Investor A</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Investor B</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Both must have identical balances because total deposits are RM8,000.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Neither can earn interest under these deposit arrangements.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Investor A's entire RM8,000 earns interest for four years. Investor B's four payments have three, two, one and zero years to compound respectively. Earlier cash flows gain more compounding time.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Equal total contributions do not imply equal future values.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q13</span><div><p>RM1,000 is invested at a nominal annual rate of 8%, compounded semiannually, for two years. What is the future value?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM1,160.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM1,166.40</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM1,169.86</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM1,188.10</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The periodic rate is 8%/2 = 4% and there are 2 x 2 = 4 periods. FV = RM1,000(1.04)^4 = RM1,169.86.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Adjust both the interest rate per period and the number of periods.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q14</span><div><p>A savings product quotes 12% nominal annual interest compounded monthly. What is the approximate effective annual rate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 12.00%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 12.36%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 13.00%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 12.68%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>EAR = (1 + 0.12/12)^12 - 1 = (1.01)^12 - 1 ≈ 12.68%. Monthly compounding creates interest on interest within the year.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not compare quoted nominal rates without accounting for compounding frequency.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q15</span><div><p>An investment compounds monthly for five years at a nominal annual interest rate of 6%. Which N and periodic interest-rate inputs are appropriate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> N = 5; I = 6%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> N = 60; I = 0.5%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> N = 60; I = 6%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> N = 12; I = 0.5%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>There are 12 periods per year for five years, so N = 60. The monthly periodic rate is 6%/12 = 0.5%. This is the setup when using a calculator's periodic-rate convention.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Never mix an annual rate with a monthly number of periods.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q16</span><div><p>An investment pays RM1,000 immediately at time zero, then RM1,000 at the end of Years 1 and 2. Which statement is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It is a mixed stream because no two payments are equal.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It is a standard ordinary annuity starting at Year 1.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It is an annuity-due pattern rather than an ordinary annuity.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It has no time-value-of-money effect because all amounts are equal.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Payments at time zero and at the beginning of subsequent periods form an annuity-due timing pattern. The appendix explicitly defines ordinary annuities as payments at period-end; applying that formula without timing adjustment would misvalue the stream.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The appendix focuses on ordinary annuities. Annuity-due terminology is an additional classification for this timing pattern.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q17</span><div><p>A financial calculator or spreadsheet treats an initial investment as a cash outflow and a future payout as a cash inflow. Which sign convention is consistent with that perspective?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Initial investment negative; future payout positive</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Both amounts negative</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Initial investment positive; future payout negative, regardless of perspective</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Both amounts must be positive</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>From the investor's perspective, committing capital is a negative cash flow and receiving proceeds is positive. Opposite signs permit financial functions to represent transfers through time consistently.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A negative PV is not necessarily a bad investment; it often indicates an initial cash outflow.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q18</span><div><p>An investment requires RM5,000 today and pays RM3,000 at the end of each of the next two years. The required return is 10%. Which decision follows present-value analysis?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Reject, because the investment has two payments rather than one.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Accept, because the PV of benefits is about RM5,206.61, exceeding the cost.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Reject, because the first RM3,000 is less than RM5,000.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Accept only if its nominal benefits exceed RM7,000.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>PV of benefits = 3,000/1.10 + 3,000/(1.10)^2 = RM5,206.61. NPV = RM5,206.61 - RM5,000 = +RM206.61. Under the assumed discount rate and cash flows, benefits exceed costs.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The decision rule is based on discounted benefits relative to present costs, not arbitrary nominal thresholds.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q19</span><div><p>The appendix's mixed-stream example pays 30, 40, 50, 60 and 70 at successive year-ends. Discounted at 8%, what is its approximate total present value?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 250.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 220.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 210.38</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 193.51</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Table 4A.5 reports individual discounted values of approximately 27.78, 34.29, 39.69, 44.10 and 47.64, adding to approximately 193.51 using the table's underlying values.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Summing the undiscounted payments produces 250, not the present value.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q20</span><div><p>Two otherwise identical investments quote the same positive nominal annual interest rate, but one compounds annually and the other monthly. Which statement is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The monthly-compounded investment has a higher effective annual rate, with diminishing incremental gains as compounding becomes increasingly frequent.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Both must have the same effective annual rate.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Annual compounding necessarily produces higher final wealth.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Increasing compounding frequency makes interest simple instead of compound.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>More frequent compounding lets accumulated interest earn interest sooner, raising the effective rate for a fixed positive nominal rate. The appendix's compounding table shows that the incremental benefit becomes smaller at higher frequencies.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The quoted rate and effective return are not identical when compounding frequencies differ.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l4p-d3\">PART B - CHAPTER 4 QUESTIONS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Section B - Chapter 4: Return and Risk (Q21-Q40)</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q21</span><div><p>A share is purchased for RM100, pays RM4 dividends during the year and ends at RM108. What is the holding-period return?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 8%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 4%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 12%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 108%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>HPR = [RM4 + (RM108 - RM100)]/RM100 = RM12/RM100 = 12%. It includes both investment income and capital appreciation.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A share's price return alone is not necessarily its total return.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q22</span><div><p>Investment A begins at RM1,000, pays RM80 income and ends at RM1,100. Investment B begins at RM1,000, pays RM120 income and ends at RM960. Which investment has the greater total return?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> B, because RM120 income exceeds RM80</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Both have identical total return because starting values are equal</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Neither can be evaluated because their income differs</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A, because its RM180 total return exceeds B's RM80</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>A earns RM80 income plus RM100 capital gain = RM180. B earns RM120 income minus RM40 capital loss = RM80. This mirrors the chapter's comparison of income and capital appreciation.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Higher cash income does not guarantee higher total return.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q23</span><div><p>An investment earns a 9% nominal annual return while inflation is 5%. Using the chapter's approximate real-return formula, what is the real return?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 14%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 4%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 9%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 3.81%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The chapter's approximation is nominal return minus inflation, giving 9% - 5% = 4%. The exact inflation-adjusted return would differ slightly, but the question explicitly requests the slide's approximation.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not confuse the approximate real-return formula with the exact Fisher adjustment.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q24</span><div><p>Suppose the real rate is 2%, expected inflation premium is 3%, and the investment-specific risk premium is 4%. What required return follows the chapter's additive model?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 9%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 7%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 5%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 4%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Required return = real rate + expected inflation premium + risk premium = 2% + 3% + 4% = 9%. The first two components sum to the risk-free rate of 5%.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not omit the inflation component or confuse the risk premium with total required return.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q25</span><div><p>An analyst forecasts a 9% expected return for an investment. Given its risk, the investor requires 12%. Which conclusion is most appropriate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The investment must be accepted because expected return is positive.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The investor must ignore the required return.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Expected compensation is inadequate relative to the required hurdle.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The asset is certain to lose 3%.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Expected return is a forecast, whereas required return is the minimum compensation demanded for risk. An expected return of 9% does not meet a 12% hurdle. This does not mean the realized return must be negative.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A shortfall against the hurdle is not a guaranteed cash loss.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q26</span><div><p>Investment A gains 40% over five years. Investment B gains 30% over one year. Neither pays interim income. Which statement is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A necessarily performed better because 40% exceeds 30%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A's annual compound return is about 6.96%, while B earned 30% in one year.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The investments' annual rates cannot be calculated.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Both investments have identical annualized performance.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A's CAGR is (1.40)^(1/5) - 1 ≈ 6.96%. B's one-year rate is 30%. Holding-period returns over different lengths should not be compared without considering time.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A larger cumulative return over a much longer period may correspond to a lower annual compound rate.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q27</span><div><p>An investment costs RM1,000 and is expected to pay RM1,400 exactly five years later, with no interim cash flows. What is its compound annual yield?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 8.00%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 40.00%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 5.00%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 6.96%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>IRR = (1,400/1,000)^(1/5) - 1 ≈ 6.96%. The 40% is the total cumulative appreciation, not the annual yield.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not divide cumulative return by five and assume that is the compound annual rate.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q28</span><div><p>A company's annual dividend rose from RM0.92 to RM1.85 over nine years. What approximate compound annual growth rate does this represent?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 8.07%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 11.23%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 101.09%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 5.56%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>g = (1.85/0.92)^(1/9) - 1 ≈ 8.07%. This is the same dividend-growth illustration developed in the chapter.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Total percentage growth and compound annual growth are different measurements.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q29</span><div><p>An investment costs RM1,000 now and produces RM600 at the end of Year 1 and RM600 at the end of Year 2. What is the approximate IRR?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 10.00%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 20.00%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 13.07%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 6.00%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Solve 1,000 = 600/(1+r) + 600/(1+r)^2. The discount rate that equates these cash flows to the investment cost is approximately 13.07%. Simply dividing the RM200 nominal surplus by the investment amount ignores the timing of both receipts.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> For a stream of cash flows, IRR requires solving a present-value equation rather than using a simple cumulative return.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q30</span><div><p>The chapter assumes interim investment income is reinvested at the original investment's IRR when interpreting a fully compounded return. If actual reinvestment opportunities earn materially less, what is the main implication?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The investment's original coupon automatically increases.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The realized terminal wealth from reinvesting income may be lower than that assumption implies.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The initial purchase price must fall to zero.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The investment necessarily becomes risk-free.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The slides emphasize interest on interest and the reinvestment rate. Earning less on interim distributions than the assumed compounding rate reduces the wealth accumulated from those distributions. This concerns the chapter's fully compounded wealth interpretation; IRR itself remains a rate solving the original cash-flow equation.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A rate calculated from cash flows is not automatically the rate available when reinvesting those cash flows.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q31</span><div><p>An investor buys shares at RM50 and still owns them when their market price reaches RM75. No shares have been sold. How should the RM25 per-share appreciation be classified?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Realized interest income</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A guaranteed dividend</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A realized capital gain from completed sale</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> An unrealized or paper capital gain</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>A paper or unrealized gain reflects appreciation in an asset still held. A realized capital gain generally arises when the position is sold above its cost, ignoring other tax-specific rules.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Unrealized does not mean the change in market value is economically imaginary.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q32</span><div><p>An investor demands a higher expected return whenever they are asked to accept greater risk. Which risk attitude best describes this investor?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Risk-averse</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Risk-indifferent</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Risk-seeking</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Risk-free</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The chapter defines a risk-averse investor as one who requires greater return to accept greater risk. Risk indifference means required return does not change with risk; risk seeking involves accepting lower return in exchange for more risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Risk aversion does not mean refusing all risky investments.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q33</span><div><p>Two otherwise comparable manufacturers face identical product demand. One has financed most operations with debt and faces large compulsory interest payments. Which risk is especially elevated for the heavily indebted firm?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Currency risk only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Liquidity risk only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Financial risk arising from capital structure</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> No additional risk because operating demand is identical</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The chapter associates financial risk with the firm's financing mix, especially debt obligations. Both companies may face similar operating or business risk, but the leveraged company has additional financial commitments.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Business risk concerns operating performance; financial risk concerns the financing structure and repayment obligations.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q34</span><div><p>An investor receives a fixed annual bond coupon. Inflation unexpectedly rises substantially while the nominal coupon remains unchanged. What risk has most directly materialized?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Only business risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Purchasing-power risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Only exchange-rate risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Underwriting risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Unexpected inflation reduces the real value of fixed nominal cash flows. The chapter identifies fixed-income securities and bank deposits as exposed to purchasing-power risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A constant cash payment may buy less over time.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q35</span><div><p>A fixed-rate bond's market value falls when market interest rates rise. Which interpretation best describes this phenomenon?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> An increase in the bond's fixed coupon</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A direct reduction in the bond's face amount</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A guarantee of issuer default</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Interest-rate risk affecting the market value of existing fixed cash flows</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>As market rates increase, existing fixed coupons become less attractive relative to newly issued debt, so the market price of the old bond generally declines. The chapter also includes unfavorable reinvestment conditions in its discussion of interest-rate risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Separate fixed promised coupons from changing market prices.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q36</span><div><p>A property valued at RM900,000 under ordinary market conditions can only be sold immediately by accepting RM650,000. Which risk is most directly illustrated?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Liquidity risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Purchasing-power risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Interest-rate risk alone</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Corporate debt risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Liquidity risk is the possibility that an asset cannot be converted to cash conveniently at a reasonable price. A substantial urgent-sale discount indicates limited market liquidity.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An asset may be valuable but difficult to sell without sacrificing price.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q37</span><div><p>A severe hurricane causes a major insurance company to report unexpected losses, immediately reducing its share price. Which risk type best describes the direct company-specific shock?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Interest-rate risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Event risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Ordinary currency conversion risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Tax risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The chapter identifies unexpected events, including major hurricanes affecting insurance companies, as examples of event risk because they cause significant and unusually immediate changes in investment value.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A sudden event affecting a particular issuer is not automatically the same as a broad market downturn.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q38</span><div><p>A broad stock market decline occurs following a major deterioration in economic conditions, affecting many companies simultaneously. Which category is most directly represented?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Company-specific product liability risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Only individual management risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Market risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Ordinary liquidity preference</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Market risk arises from broader market factors independent of any single investment, such as economy-wide downturns or political upheaval. The same event can influence many security returns.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Differentiate broad economic exposure from a shock unique to one company.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q39</span><div><p>A Malaysian investor holds foreign securities. Their MYR value declines due to exchange-rate movement, and a new unfavorable tax rule lowers after-tax investment returns. Which two risk categories are illustrated?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Business risk and financial risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Event risk and liquidity risk exclusively</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Interest-rate risk and underwriting risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Currency exchange risk and tax risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Exchange-rate changes influence the home-currency value of foreign holdings, while unfavorable tax-law changes can reduce after-tax returns and security valuations. The chapter identifies these as separate sources of risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The same investment can be affected by several risk sources at once.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q40</span><div><p>Investment A has an average return of 10% and standard deviation of 20%. Investment B has an average return of 20% and standard deviation of 30%. Assuming positive average returns, which statement is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A has lower absolute volatility, but B has lower relative dispersion measured by CV.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> B has lower absolute volatility and higher CV.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Both investments have the same coefficient of variation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A has lower coefficient of variation because its standard deviation is smaller.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A has σ = 20% and CV = 20/10 = 2.0. B has σ = 30% and CV = 30/20 = 1.5. A has lower total standard deviation, while B has less standard deviation per unit of average return.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Standard deviation measures absolute dispersion; coefficient of variation evaluates relative dispersion.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l4p-d5\">PART C - FORMULAS AND REVIEW</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Essential formula sheet</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l4_tvm_timeline\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>FV and PV are the same equation run in opposite directions along the timeline.</figcaption></figure></div><p>You should recognize these formulas from the question's economic structure rather than memorizing them in isolation.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Formula</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Lump-sum FV</td>\n<td>$FV=PV(1+r)^n$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lump-sum PV</td>\n<td>$PV=\\frac{FV}{(1+r)^n}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FV of ordinary annuity</td>\n<td>$PMT\\frac{(1+r)^n-1}{r}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PV of ordinary annuity</td>\n<td>$PMT\\frac{1-(1+r)^{-n}}{r}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PV of mixed stream</td>\n<td>$\\sum_{t=1}^{n}\\frac{CF_t}{(1+r)^t}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Effective annual rate</td>\n<td>$(1+\\frac{r}{m})^m-1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HPR</td>\n<td>$\\frac{\\text{Income}+P_1-P_0}{P_0}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Required return</td>\n<td>$R_f+\\text{Risk premium}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Approximate real return</td>\n<td>Nominal return - inflation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CAGR</td>\n<td>$(FV/PV)^{1/n}-1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRR for cash-flow stream</td>\n<td>Solve $0=-P_0+\\sum\\frac{CF_t}{(1+IRR)^t}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Coefficient of variation</td>\n<td>$CV=\\frac{\\sigma}{\\bar R}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">How to interpret your exam mistakes</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Mistake pattern</th>\n<th>What you need to revisit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>PV versus FV</td>\n<td>Which direction on the timeline are you moving?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Simple versus compound interest</td>\n<td>Does previously earned interest earn additional interest?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ordinary annuity versus mixed stream</td>\n<td>Are payments equal, regularly spaced and at period-end?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compounding frequency</td>\n<td>Did you adjust both the rate and number of periods?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HPR versus IRR</td>\n<td>Are you measuring total return or annualized compound return?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Expected versus required return</td>\n<td>Forecast versus minimum acceptable compensation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Business versus financial risk</td>\n<td>Operating uncertainty versus financing obligations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Interest-rate versus purchasing-power risk</td>\n<td>Changing market rates versus changing real value of money</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Standard deviation versus CV</td>\n<td>Absolute dispersion versus relative dispersion</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A high-level MCQ exam often gives you all the numbers necessary for a calculation, but the difficult part is deciding which calculation is economically meaningful.</p>\n<p>For example, comparing RM100,000 today with RM140,000 five years later is not fundamentally an arithmetic problem. It is a time-value problem. Likewise, comparing two investments with 10% and 15% expected returns is not simply choosing the larger number; it is a risk-compensation problem.</p>\n<p>If you can identify the economic problem before selecting a formula, you've understood the two chapters at a much deeper level.</p>\n<p>Source note: Questions are based on Chapter 4 and Appendix 4A, with original numerical scenarios alongside selected textbook examples. Some descriptions reflect the textbook's simplified financial models; real-world investment decisions may require additional assumptions, fees, taxation and risk adjustments.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"question-bank-wrap hard-wrap\" id=\"l4-hard\"><summary><div><strong>Difficult mode - 30 questions</strong><small>18 Bloom-graded questions (3 per level) and 12 marked extension questions. Answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><p class=\"hard-intro\">Questions climb Bloom&#39;s taxonomy from <b>Remember</b> to <b>Create</b>. The <b>extension</b> questions go beyond the slides (industry practice, CFA-style, case competitions, Master&#39;s, PhD and postdoc level) but can be reasoned out from the chapter&#39;s principles.</p><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn\" data-quiz=\"4\" data-quiz-mode=\"difficult\">Start Chapter 4 difficult mode</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"4\" data-quiz-mode=\"extensions\">Extensions only</button></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which statement defines the time value of money?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Money loses value only when prices rise.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> As long as money can earn interest, its value depends on the point in time at which it is received.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Interest is paid only on the original deposit.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Cash flows in the future are always larger than cash flows today.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A dollar (or ringgit) received sooner can start earning interest sooner, so it is worth more than the same amount later. A describes inflation, C simple interest, and D is not true in general.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which equation gives the <strong>required return</strong> in the chapter's model?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Required return = real rate x inflation premium x risk premium</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Required return = real rate + expected inflation premium + risk premium</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Required return = expected return - risk premium</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Required return = risk-free rate - risk premium</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The three components add. Because the risk-free rate equals real rate plus inflation premium, this simplifies to R<sub>f</sub> + risk premium.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>What does the <strong>coefficient of variation</strong> measure?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Absolute dispersion of returns</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The sensitivity of a stock to the market</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The slope of the security market line</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Relative dispersion: standard deviation per unit of average return</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>CV = σ / mean return. Standard deviation is the absolute measure (A); beta and the SML belong to Chapter 5.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Why does the present value of a future payment fall when the discount rate rises?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because the payment itself shrinks.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because inflation rises with the discount rate.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because a higher opportunity rate means less money today is needed to grow into the same future amount.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because compounding becomes simple interest.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>PV = FV / (1 + r)<sup>n</sup>. A larger r makes the denominator larger. Economically, if you can earn more elsewhere, you need to put less aside now.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Why can holding-period return (HPR) mislead when the holding period exceeds a year?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> HPR does not account for the time value of money, so returns over different horizons are not comparable.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> HPR ignores capital gains.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> HPR only works for bonds.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> HPR cannot be negative.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A 40% return over one year and a 40% return over five years have the same HPR but very different annual performance. Compound annual yield (IRR) fixes this.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Which pair correctly contrasts business risk and financial risk?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Business risk comes from interest rates; financial risk comes from inflation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Business risk affects bonds only; financial risk affects stocks only.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Business risk concerns uncertainty in the firm's earnings and operations; financial risk concerns uncertainty from its debt-and-equity financing mix.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> They are the same risk described differently.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Two identical restaurants share similar business risk, but the one with heavy debt carries more financial risk.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>RM5,000 is deposited for 4 years at 6% compounded annually. What is the future value?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM6,200.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM6,312.38</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM6,000.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM6,524.77</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>FV = 5,000 x (1.06)<sup>4</sup> = 5,000 x 1.262477 = <strong>RM6,312.38</strong>. A uses simple interest-like growth; C is simple interest at 5%; D uses 7%.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>What is the present value of RM10,000 due in 3 years at an 8% discount rate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM7,938.32</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM7,500.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM8,000.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM8,264.46</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>PV = 10,000 / 1.08<sup>3</sup> = 10,000 / 1.259712 = <strong>RM7,938.32</strong>. D discounts for only 2 years.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>A share is bought at RM80, pays a RM4 dividend, and is worth RM84 at year-end. What is the HPR?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 5%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 10%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 15%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 4%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>HPR = [income + (P<sub>1</sub> - P<sub>0</sub>)] / P<sub>0</sub> = (4 + 4) / 80 = <strong>10%</strong>. A counts only the dividend or only the gain.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>RM1,000 is deposited at the end of each year for 8 years at 8% (future value RM10,636.63). Which statement is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Interest earned is RM2,636.63, because earlier deposits compound for longer.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Interest earned is RM8,000.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The future value equals RM8,000 x 1.08.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The last deposit earns seven years of interest.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Total deposits = RM8,000, so interest = 10,636.63 - 8,000 = <strong>RM2,636.63</strong>. The first deposit compounds seven years and the last (made at the valuation date) earns nothing.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>Investment A has a mean return of 10% and σ of 20%. Investment B has a mean of 20% and σ of 30%. Which is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A is riskier on both measures.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A has lower σ, but B has lower relative dispersion (CV 1.5 versus 2.0).</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> B has lower σ and lower CV.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> CV cannot be computed for these data.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>CV(A) = 20 / 10 = 2.0; CV(B) = 30 / 20 = 1.5. Absolute and relative risk can rank investments differently.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>An asset is bought for RM1,000 and sold for RM1,400 five years later, with no income. Which statement is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Its compound annual return is 8%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Its compound annual return is 40%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Its HPR is 40%, and its compound annual return is about 6.96%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Its HPR is 6.96%.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>HPR = 400 / 1,000 = 40%. Annual yield = 1.4<sup>1/5</sup> - 1 ≈ 6.96%. Dividing 40% by 5 (= 8%) ignores compounding.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D13</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>Evaluate: \"An investment expected to return 12% is attractive, because 12% is a good return.\"</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Incomplete: it depends on the required return for its risk; if the required return is 14%, 12% is inadequate.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Correct.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Correct if the investment is a stock.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Incorrect, because only returns above 20% are attractive.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Expected return is a forecast; required return is the hurdle. No return is good or bad in isolation.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D14</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>Evaluate: \"Investment B has the lower standard deviation, so it is the better investment.\"</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Incomplete: low risk is not automatically superior if the return is far lower; the decision depends on required return, risk preference and objectives.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Correct.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Incorrect, because σ is irrelevant.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Correct if B is a bond.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The chapter teaches optimising the relationship between return and risk, not minimising risk.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D15</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>Bank A offers 12.5% compounded annually. Bank B offers 12% compounded monthly. Which is better?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A, since 12.5% &gt; 12%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> B, since monthly compounding always beats annual compounding.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> They are equal.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> B: its effective annual rate is (1.01)<sup>12</sup> - 1 ≈ 12.68%, above A's 12.5%.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Compare effective annual rates, not stated rates. Option D overgeneralises: monthly compounding beats annual compounding only at the same stated rate; here the stated rates differ, so the calculation decides.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D16</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>You need RM50,000 in 5 years and can earn 8% a year. Which equal year-end contribution is required (approximately)?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM10,000</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM6,250</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM7,500</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM8,523</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>FV annuity factor = (1.08<sup>5</sup> - 1) / 0.08 = 5.8666. PMT = 50,000 / 5.8666 ≈ <strong>RM8,523</strong>. A ignores interest.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D17</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>Which sequence best builds a decision framework for choosing among three investments, per the chapter?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Choose the highest expected return.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Choose the lowest standard deviation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Estimate expected returns; determine the required return for each risk; measure risk with SD and CV; compare expected with required return; choose the one consistent with the investor's risk preference.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Choose the one with the longest history.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>This is the chapter's four-step decision process, combining return, risk and investor preference.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D18</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>An investor estimates a real return of 2.5%, expected inflation of 3% and a risk premium of 4% for a particular security. What required return should the investor use as the discount rate or hurdle?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 5.5%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 7.5%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 4.0%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 9.5%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>R<sub>f</sub> = 2.5% + 3% = 5.5%; required return = 5.5% + 4% = <strong>9.5%</strong>. A is the risk-free rate only.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension questions</span><small>beyond the notes, lowest Bloom level first</small></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>A retiree holds a bond paying a fixed RM5,000 a year while inflation rises sharply. Which risk is most directly at work?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Business risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Purchasing-power risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Event risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Financial risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The nominal payment is unchanged, but its real value falls. Fixed-income securities and deposits are the chapter's examples of purchasing-power risk.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>A company borrows RM100,000 at 8% and repays it in 5 equal year-end instalments. Approximately what is each instalment?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM25,046</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM20,000</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM28,000</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM21,600</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The loan is the present value of an ordinary annuity: PMT = 100,000 / [(1 - 1.08<sup>-5</sup>) / 0.08] = 100,000 / 3.99271 ≈ <strong>RM25,046</strong>.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · CFA</span></div><p>A stated rate of 8% is compounded quarterly. What is the effective annual rate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 8.00%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 8.50%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 8.24%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 8.32%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>(1 + 0.08/4)<sup>4</sup> - 1 = 1.02<sup>4</sup> - 1 = 1.082432 - 1 ≈ <strong>8.24%</strong>. More frequent compounding raises the true rate.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · CFA</span></div><p>At 9% compounded annually, roughly how long does money take to double?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> About 4 years</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> About 16 years</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> About 12 years</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> About 8 years</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Solve 1.09<sup>n</sup> = 2: n = ln 2 / ln 1.09 ≈ 8.04 years. (The \"rule of 72\" gives 72 / 9 = 8.)</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Case competition</span></div><p>An investment costs RM12,000 and pays RM4,000, RM5,000 and RM6,000 at the ends of years 1-3. At a 10% required return its NPV is about +RM276. What happens at a 12% required return?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> NPV rises, because the payments are unchanged.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> NPV stays exactly the same.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> NPV turns negative (PV of benefits ≈ RM11,828 &lt; RM12,000), reversing the decision.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The investment becomes risk-free.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>At 12%: 4,000/1.12 + 5,000/1.12<sup>2</sup> + 6,000/1.12<sup>3</sup> ≈ 3,571 + 3,986 + 4,271 = 11,828. A thin positive NPV has little room for adverse changes in assumptions.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>Why does using IRR to evaluate a bond implicitly assume reinvestment of coupons at the IRR itself?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because the IRR solves for one single rate that discounts every cash flow, which is equivalent to compounding each interim cash flow forward at that same rate.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because coupons are always spent.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because IRR ignores coupons.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because reinvestment rates are fixed by law.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The chapter flags this assumption: if actual reinvestment rates are lower, the fully compounded return falls below the IRR.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>An investment returns +50% in year 1 and -50% in year 2. The arithmetic average return is 0%. What happens to wealth?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It is unchanged.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It rises by 25%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It falls to 75% of the starting amount, a compound annual return of about -13.4%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It falls to 50% of the starting amount.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>1.5 x 0.5 = 0.75. The compound (geometric) annual return is √0.75 - 1 ≈ -13.4%. This is why averages of annual returns overstate compound growth when returns are volatile.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Case competition</span></div><p>Fund A gained 50% over 4 years. Fund B earned 11% a year for 4 years. Which is better on a compound annual basis?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> B: A's compound annual return is 1.5<sup>1/4</sup> - 1 ≈ 10.67%, below B's 11%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A, because 50% is larger than 11%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> They are equal.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A, because it earned its return faster.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Always annualise before comparing horizons.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>Evaluate: \"A 10-year historical standard deviation tells us exactly how volatile the investment will be next year.\"</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Correct.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Overstated: it is an estimate from a limited sample that can change; it is evidence for forming expectations, not a promise.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Correct for stocks but not for bonds.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Incorrect because standard deviation is always zero.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The chapter says historical performance provides a basis for expectations but not a guarantee. A five-observation sample, as in the Target example, offers limited evidence.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>When is the coefficient of variation a poor measure?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> When returns are always positive.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> When the mean return is near zero or negative, so the ratio becomes huge, undefined or hard to interpret.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> When there are more than 10 observations.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> When returns are in ringgit.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>CV = σ / mean. A mean near zero makes CV explode; a negative mean makes its sign meaningless. Use σ and judgement in such cases.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>Textbooks use the 3-month Treasury bill as the risk-free rate. When discounting a 25-year project, what is the main conceptual concern?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> T-bills pay no interest.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> No concern: the risk-free rate never changes.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> T-bills are riskier than stocks.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A maturity mismatch: a short-term rate reflects short-term conditions, so the discount rate for long cash flows should reflect longer-horizon rates and the project's own risk.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The risk-free rate is a proxy. Required return also needs a risk premium appropriate to the project and the horizon.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>Which exam question best separates a student who memorised formulas from one who understands TVM?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> \"State the PV formula.\"</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> \"Define an annuity.\"</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> \"What does PMT stand for?\"</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> \"Two projects pay RM300 a year for 5 years starting now, versus RM300 a year for 5 years starting in one year. Without a calculator, which is worth more at a positive rate, and why? Then explain how the two values relate.\"</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>It requires recognising an annuity due versus an ordinary annuity, and reasoning that every payment arrives one period earlier, so the annuity due is worth (1 + r) times as much.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div></details>");
